xline
=====

xKit's line-editing module. Forked from isocline v1.1.0 by Daan Leijen
(https://github.com/daanx/isocline), licensed under the MIT License
(see LICENSE.isocline).

Why a fork
----------
Upstream isocline is tiny, portable (POSIX + native Win32 Console),
pure-C, and BSD-licensed. It is the ideal starting point for xKit's
REPL layer. We vendor it rather than consuming it via FetchContent
so that we can:

  1. Integrate directly with xKit's event loop (xLoop) — upstream
     only exposes a blocking `ic_readline()`; we need
     begin/step/end driven by our poller.
  2. Drop the `mem_t` / `alloc_t` indirection and use the standard
     allocator directly (or, later, an xKit-specific allocator).
  3. Rename the public API (`ic_*`, `IC_*`) to xKit's conventions
     (`xLine*`, `XLINE_*`).

Upstream synchronisation
------------------------
We do NOT track upstream. Each change here is a deliberate
modification; cherry-picking upstream fixes is a manual operation.
The original v1.1.0 snapshot landed in the first commit of branch
`codebuddy/xline` so the diff from upstream is always reviewable.

Layout
------
  line.c                  — public API entry points (xLine*), wired
                            on top of the internal helpers. Formerly
                            isocline.c; renamed to match xKit's
                            module/header naming (line.h ⇄ line.c).
  line.h                  — public header. Exposes the xLine* /
                            XLINE_* names consumed by xKit.
  {attr,bbcode,...}.[ch]  — internal implementation. Each .c is its
                            own translation unit; the former
                            `#if !defined(IC_SEPARATE_OBJS)` umbrella
                            has been retired. Internal names
                            (ic_private, ic_env_t, ic_get_env, etc.)
                            are intentionally kept upstream-style to
                            minimise drift when cherry-picking fixes.

